      <h3>Domain-Driven German Language Learning Platform</h3>

      <div class="purple-squirrel-box" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        <h4>Architectural Decision Record (ADR)</h4>
        <p>The user's current learning state is formalized as a typed Zod schema. <strong>The AI's behavior is governed by that state. Not by assumptions about it.</strong></p>

        <div class="adr-code-block">
          <span style="color:#8a7f87;">// Pedagogical Domain Contract</span><br/>
          <span style="color:#FF6B35;">export const</span> WochenKontextSchema = z.object({"{"}<br/>
          &nbsp;&nbsp;gelernt:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; z.array(z.string()),&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:#8a7f87;">// structures taught</span><br/>
          &nbsp;&nbsp;nicht_gelernt: z.array(z.string()),&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:#8a7f87;">// not yet taught</span><br/>
          &nbsp;&nbsp;korrektur:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; KorrekturSchema&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style="color:#8a7f87;">// what AI can / cannot correct</span><br/>
          {"}"});
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="project-body">
        The platform ran for over a decade with the pedagogical boundary — what the AI was
        allowed to act on — living in the teacher's head, not the system. In 2024, students
        began reporting confusion from AI feedback on grammar they had not yet studied. This was
        not a bug. It was a missing domain contract. WochenKontextSchema formalizes that contract:
        the AI is architecturally forbidden from correcting grammar the student has not been taught.
        The user's current state governs the system. Not the other way around.
        <br/><br/>
        The same principle applies to your onboarding flow. When your system's behavior is
        governed by assumptions about what the user already knows — rather than by what the user's
        current state actually allows — that is an architecture failure. That is what the audit finds.
      </p>

